@page "/About"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Sections

<PageTitle>About Sleeping Gods Companion</PageTitle>

<SectionContent SectionName="page-title">About</SectionContent>

<h3>About</h3>

<p>
	The Sleeping Gods UNOFFICIAL Companion App.  I built this app to save my poor eyes scribbling notes
	on the back of the campaign sheet and trying to work out what was useful information to store.  We
	upgraded to a notepad but I felt this would be smarter.
</p>

<p>
	This application is not sponsored or endorsed by the creators of Sleeping Gods or Red Raven Games.
	Any imagry from the game remain the exclusive copyright of author.
</p>

<p>
	I do not have the expansion, so I can't put the related assets into the app. If you have the expansion,
	and can provide me with some details, I'm happy to add.
</p>

<p>
	The app is released under MIT licence and the source can be found on <a href="https://github.com/Dash/Ox.SleepingGods" target="_blank">GitHub</a>.
</p>

<h3>Installation</h3>
<p>This is a 'Progressive Web App', meaning you can install it as an app onto your computer or phone for offline use.
	Look for the Add App icon in your browser to install.
</p>

<h3>Usage</h3>
<p>Pick your location form the home page, and fill in details about the location, including any keywords required.</p>
<p>You can click into a keyword to set more information about it, and see where else it is used or found.</p>
<p>Keywords/Quests only show if you've found them, to retain the mystery.</p>
<p>When challenges and rewards are gated at a location by a keyword, it's best to click into the keyword
	and add them there.  This way you can identify what is available merely by exploring, and what else happens with
	the keyword.
</p>
<p>
	Keywords can be marked as standard or a quest, with the former tending to lock you out of places already visited
	and tend to be found where they're needed.  Mark favourite quests for remembering to revisit in future campaigns.
</p>

<h3>Backing up and sharing</h3>
<p>The data you enter is saved into your local browser's cache.  This <i>can</i> be evicted by your browser,
	wiped if you clear it, and obviously not available on other devices.</p>
<p>To backup your data, go to the Maintenance tab and run an export.  You can later import the resulting
	JSON file in the future, or on another device.  An import will wipe your current database and replace it -
	be sure to backup yours before importing other peoples!
</p>

<h3>Server Sync</h3>
<p>You can opt to synchronise your local database with a remote server. You will need to run your own sync server
	to specification to enable this (i.e. one doesn't come with this app).  Sample implementations are available in
	the source code repositories.</p>

<p>The following considerations apply when using a synchronisation server:
	<ul>
		<li>Syncs are only performed on updates and when the app starts up (or the browser is hard reloaded).</li>
		<li>Enabling sync may incur a delay on app start while the sync is performed (network speed and db size dependent).</li>
		<li>The local database identifier scopes the sync, if you reset your database you will lose access to your file.</li>
		<li>For the same reason, if you want to use your database elsewhere, you must first import it manually into the other device.</li>
		<li>Using more than one device at the same time is unsupported, there is no concurrency checking, and the latest version
			always wins out.
		</li>
		<li>When importing a database, the sync url will always be blanked out to avoid accidenally overwriting a newer
			copy from another device.
		</li>
		<li>If you're offline then make sure you load the app as soon as you get data to ensure your copy is pushed.</li>
		<li>You must use a https endpoint when loading this app over https.</li>
	</ul>
</p>

<p>The basic premise of the sync mechanism is to allow you to more automatically share your latest database with somebody
	else after your game session.  If you have multiple devices try to update simultaneously, things will go wrong and
	changes will be lost.
</p>

